Rice flour is produced by milling rice grains into a fine powder and is widely used in noodles, pasta, bakery products, sweets, desserts, and food-thickening applications. Its naturally gluten-free composition makes it an alternative to wheat flour for consumers seeking gluten-free products. Manufacturers also value its neutral taste and functional properties, which support its use in diverse food formulations.

Asia-Pacific remains the largest regional market, supported by established rice-based culinary traditions and a strong production base. Meanwhile, demand in other regions is expanding as consumers explore gluten-free baking, specialty food products, and alternative ingredients. Manufacturers are broadening their portfolios to include brown rice flour, specialty blends, and products designed for specific nutritional and processing requirements.

The market is also evolving through improvements in milling technology, sustainable sourcing initiatives, and the expansion of online retail. These developments are creating opportunities for producers to improve product consistency, reach niche consumer groups, and strengthen supply-chain transparency. However, fluctuations in rice prices and differences in regional preferences continue to influence production costs and commercial strategies.

